# Line endings.
#
# core.autocrlf=true on this team's Windows machines, so anything checked out
# gets CRLF unless told otherwise. For a shell script that is fatal and the
# error does not name the cause: bash reads the `\r` as part of the command and
# reports `$'\r': command not found`, or dies on the shebang.
#
# Tri hit exactly that running scripts/amplify_recover.sh and had to strip the
# CRs by hand. Marking the file types instead means nobody has to do it again.

*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# Same reason: the Amplify build reads this with `nvm use $(cat .nvmrc)`, and a
# trailing CR makes it ask for a version named "22.18.0\r".
.nvmrc text eol=lf

# Anything with a shebang but no extension lives here too.
scripts/*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts want CRLF.
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Lockfile never auto-merges.
#
# @aws-amplify/*-construct ship bundled deps, and npm serialises a bundled tree
# differently between npm versions - one writes the nested @opentelemetry/core
# entries, another hoists and drops them. Both sides then look like valid
# lockfiles, so `ort` picks one silently and npm ci dies on the other machine.
#
# That is what broke Amplify deployment #21: a merge took the side missing
# @opentelemetry/core@2.0.0, which @opentelemetry/resources@2.0.0 pins exactly.
# Forcing a conflict makes whoever merges choose on purpose.

ECA_UI/frontend/package-lock.json merge=binary
